From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 17:16:28 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] usb: dwc3: apple: keep a T8132 host's devices through a deep
 sleep

Every deep sleep (mem with apple-s2r, pm_suspend_via_firmware()) makes
every USB device on a T8132 port enumerate again: a mounted disk is gone
after the wake, a keyboard comes back as a new device. macOS keeps them.

The cause is in dwc3_apple_pm_notify(): at PM_SUSPEND_PREPARE of a deep
sleep a host sleeps in place only behind a fixed hub on T8140, where the
PHY can hibernate the core. Anything else is stopped the way a cable
disconnect stops it, dwc3_apple_exit() unregistering xHCI and with it
every device, and started again at PM_POST_SUSPEND. A T8132 PHY has no
controller power control, so every J604 port took the stop.

The host can stay registered instead. xHCI already copes with a
controller that lost its power: a positive return from the glue's
pre_host_resume tells xhci_resume() so, and it resets and starts the
controller over while the USB core reset-resumes the devices
(USB_DEFAULT_PERSIST). What is missing is a core that xHCI can start:
the PHY and the core went down with the SoC, and the core may sit in
reset.

So for a T8132 host, with the PHY's agreement
(apple_atcphy_sleep_lose_prepare(), a plain USB mode with nothing half
done), the glue now leaves xHCI registered and writes its sleep GUID as
for a sleep in place. At the resume, before xHCI touches the core:

 - a sleep that didn't reach its commit (no pm_resume_via_firmware())
   kept the power, and xHCI resumes as usual;
 - otherwise the PHY is set up again (apple_atcphy_sleep_lost()), and the
   core is brought up the way dwc3_apple_core_start() and
   dwc3_apple_set_role() bring up a T8132 host on a cable attach: reset
   asserted, USB2 host role latched, reset released, core initialised
   (with the PHY references it holds kept), CIO setup, USB3 PIPE routed
   with the T8132 U3 rule. xHCI is told it lost its state and starts
   over. The core is not read before its reset is released.

Any failure keeps xHCI away from the core, and PM_POST_SUSPEND restarts
the host as before (the devices enumerate again); a core that could not
be released from reset is left alone, USB off until a reboot, as for a
lost sleep in place. A role change during the sleep is held and taken up
after it, as for a stopped controller. Gadget mode, a role other than
host, and the other generations keep their paths. persist_sleep=0
returns T8132 to the stop.

The sleep choice is a pure helper in dwc3-apple-state.h with KUnit
cases (who asks the PHY, the plan, a role gone between the PHY's answer
and the lock).

TO-VERIFY on the J604 (no deep sleep there yet: a bound dcpext and the
GPU still refuse it), dev-loop check on a kboot-s2r boot after
`echo mem > /sys/power/state`:
 - a USB stick and a USB3 disk keep their device paths and numbers
   (lsusb -t, /sys/bus/usb/devices/*/devnum before and after), a mounted
   filesystem on it stays mounted and readable;
 - dmesg shows "power lost in the sleep: host set up again" and
   "reset ... USB device number N" for each device (hub.c, same N), no
   "new ... USB device number";
 - the disk links at 5000M after the wake (lsusb -t);
 - with persist_sleep=0 the old restart comes back.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5.5
Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
---
 drivers/usb/dwc3/dwc3-apple-state-test.c |  38 +++-
 drivers/usb/dwc3/dwc3-apple-state.h      |  41 ++++
 drivers/usb/dwc3/dwc3-apple.c            | 226 ++++++++++++++++++++---
 3 files changed, 279 insertions(+), 26 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/usb/dwc3/dwc3-apple-state-test.c b/drivers/usb/dwc3/dwc3-apple-state-test.c
index 69abb0f326..b48ea9e81c 100644
--- a/drivers/usb/dwc3/dwc3-apple-state-test.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/dwc3/dwc3-apple-state-test.c
@@ -46,11 +46,47 @@ static void dwc3_apple_role_during_sleep_test(struct kunit *test)
 	KUNIT_EXPECT_EQ(test, dwc3_apple_state_for_role(USB_ROLE_NONE), DWC3_APPLE_NO_CABLE);
 }
 
+/* Only a host that doesn't sleep in place, with the parameter on, asks the PHY. */
+static void dwc3_apple_sleep_may_lose_test(struct kunit *test)
+{
+	KUNIT_EXPECT_TRUE(test, dwc3_apple_sleep_may_lose(DWC3_APPLE_HOST, false, true));
+	KUNIT_EXPECT_FALSE(test, dwc3_apple_sleep_may_lose(DWC3_APPLE_HOST, true, true));
+	KUNIT_EXPECT_FALSE(test, dwc3_apple_sleep_may_lose(DWC3_APPLE_HOST, false, false));
+	KUNIT_EXPECT_FALSE(test, dwc3_apple_sleep_may_lose(DWC3_APPLE_DEVICE, false, true));
+	KUNIT_EXPECT_FALSE(test, dwc3_apple_sleep_may_lose(DWC3_APPLE_NO_CABLE, false, true));
+	KUNIT_EXPECT_FALSE(test, dwc3_apple_sleep_may_lose(DWC3_APPLE_PROBE_PENDING, false, true));
+}
+
+/* In place comes first; a host the PHY agreed to lose reset-resumes; anything else stops. */
+static void dwc3_apple_sleep_plan_test(struct kunit *test)
+{
+	KUNIT_EXPECT_EQ(test, dwc3_apple_sleep_plan(DWC3_APPLE_HOST, true, false),
+			DWC3_APPLE_SLEEP_IN_PLACE);
+	KUNIT_EXPECT_EQ(test, dwc3_apple_sleep_plan(DWC3_APPLE_HOST, false, true),
+			DWC3_APPLE_SLEEP_RESET_RESUME);
+	KUNIT_EXPECT_EQ(test, dwc3_apple_sleep_plan(DWC3_APPLE_HOST, false, false),
+			DWC3_APPLE_SLEEP_STOP);
+	KUNIT_EXPECT_EQ(test, dwc3_apple_sleep_plan(DWC3_APPLE_DEVICE, false, false),
+			DWC3_APPLE_SLEEP_STOP);
+}
+
+/* A role that went away between the PHY's answer and the glue's lock is stopped. */
+static void dwc3_apple_sleep_plan_role_gone_test(struct kunit *test)
+{
+	KUNIT_EXPECT_EQ(test, dwc3_apple_sleep_plan(DWC3_APPLE_NO_CABLE, false, true),
+			DWC3_APPLE_SLEEP_STOP);
+	KUNIT_EXPECT_EQ(test, dwc3_apple_sleep_plan(DWC3_APPLE_DEVICE, false, true),
+			DWC3_APPLE_SLEEP_STOP);
+}
+
 static struct kunit_case dwc3_apple_state_test_cases[] = {
 	KUNIT_CASE(dwc3_apple_host_left_to_xhci_test),
 	KUNIT_CASE(dwc3_apple_device_selected_before_reset_test),
 	KUNIT_CASE(dwc3_apple_non_role_test),
 	KUNIT_CASE(dwc3_apple_role_during_sleep_test),
+	KUNIT_CASE(dwc3_apple_sleep_may_lose_test),
+	KUNIT_CASE(dwc3_apple_sleep_plan_test),
+	KUNIT_CASE(dwc3_apple_sleep_plan_role_gone_test),
 	{}
 };
 
@@ -61,5 +97,5 @@ static struct kunit_suite dwc3_apple_state_test_suite = {
 
 kunit_test_suite(dwc3_apple_state_test_suite);
 
-MODULE_DESCRIPTION("KUnit tests for the Apple Silicon DWC3 glue USB2 mode choice");
+MODULE_DESCRIPTION("KUnit tests for the Apple Silicon DWC3 glue mode and sleep choices");
 MODULE_LICENSE("GPL");
diff --git a/drivers/usb/dwc3/dwc3-apple-state.h b/drivers/usb/dwc3/dwc3-apple-state.h
index bb8d827fde..74445ea205 100644
--- a/drivers/usb/dwc3/dwc3-apple-state.h
+++ b/drivers/usb/dwc3/dwc3-apple-state.h
@@ -66,4 +66,45 @@ static inline enum dwc3_apple_state dwc3_apple_state_for_role(enum usb_role role
 	}
 }
 
+/* How the controller goes through a deep sleep, which may cut its power. */
+enum dwc3_apple_sleep_plan {
+	DWC3_APPLE_SLEEP_IN_PLACE, /* The PHY hibernates the core, xHCI stays (T8140 fixed hub) */
+	DWC3_APPLE_SLEEP_RESET_RESUME, /* xHCI stays and starts over at the resume (T8132 host) */
+	DWC3_APPLE_SLEEP_STOP, /* Stopped before the sleep and started after it */
+};
+
+/**
+ * dwc3_apple_sleep_may_lose() - whether to ask the PHY to set a host up again after a power loss
+ * @state: the controller's state
+ * @in_place: the host sleeps in place already
+ * @persist: the persist_sleep module parameter
+ *
+ * Only a host has devices that can reset-resume; a gadget is stopped as before.
+ *
+ * Return: true if the PHY is to be asked.
+ */
+static inline bool dwc3_apple_sleep_may_lose(enum dwc3_apple_state state, bool in_place,
+					     bool persist)
+{
+	return persist && !in_place && state == DWC3_APPLE_HOST;
+}
+
+/**
+ * dwc3_apple_sleep_plan() - how the controller goes through a deep sleep
+ * @state: the controller's state, read again under the glue's lock
+ * @in_place: the PHY takes the core through hibernation
+ * @lose: the PHY agreed to set a host up again after it lost its power
+ *
+ * Return: the plan. A role that went away after the PHY agreed falls back to the stop.
+ */
+static inline enum dwc3_apple_sleep_plan
+dwc3_apple_sleep_plan(enum dwc3_apple_state state, bool in_place, bool lose)
+{
+	if (in_place)
+		return DWC3_APPLE_SLEEP_IN_PLACE;
+	if (lose && state == DWC3_APPLE_HOST)
+		return DWC3_APPLE_SLEEP_RESET_RESUME;
+	return DWC3_APPLE_SLEEP_STOP;
+}
+
 #endif /* __DWC3_APPLE_STATE_H */
diff --git a/drivers/usb/dwc3/dwc3-apple.c b/drivers/usb/dwc3/dwc3-apple.c
index 1cbffc6d02..ba99020a1c 100644
--- a/drivers/usb/dwc3/dwc3-apple.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/dwc3/dwc3-apple.c
@@ -99,9 +99,12 @@
  * sleeps in place, as macOS's does: xHCI suspends and resumes as usual, and the PHY takes the
  * core through hibernation in between, so the hub and its devices stay. A core that comes
  * back without its registers is set up again in place and xHCI starts over, so the devices
- * reset-resume. Anywhere else, or when the PHY can't, the glue stops everything before the
- * freezer runs, the way a cable disconnect does, and brings it back once the system has
- * resumed. See dwc3_apple_pm_notify().
+ * reset-resume. A T8132 host has no hibernation, but stays registered across the sleep all
+ * the same: at the resume the PHY is set up again, the core is brought up in the order of a
+ * cable attach and xHCI starts over, so its devices reset-resume too, as macOS keeps them (see
+ * dwc3_apple_t8132_reset_resume(); the persist_sleep parameter turns it off). Anywhere else,
+ * or when the PHY can't, the glue stops everything before the freezer runs, the way a cable
+ * disconnect does, and brings it back once the system has resumed. See dwc3_apple_pm_notify().
  *
  * Runtime PM (T6040 and T8132, see dwc3_apple_core_probe()): the glue holds a reference from
  * the reset deassert to the end of the shutdown, so with no cable the controller can
@@ -171,6 +174,12 @@ struct dwc3_apple {
 	u32 sleep_guid;
 	/* The core lost its registers in that sleep and was set up again in place. */
 	bool sleep_reinit;
+	/*
+	 * A T8132 host kept registered through a deep sleep that may cut its power, and whether
+	 * the resume found the power gone. See dwc3_apple_t8132_reset_resume().
+	 */
+	bool sleep_reset_resume;
+	bool sleep_lost;
 	/* Looks for the fixed hub once a host restarted after a sleep has had time to find it. */
 	struct delayed_work hub_check;
 	struct work_struct sleep_finish;
@@ -185,6 +194,11 @@ struct dwc3_apple {
 
 #define to_dwc3_apple(d) container_of((d), struct dwc3_apple, dwc)
 
+static bool persist_sleep = true;
+module_param(persist_sleep, bool, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(persist_sleep,
+		 "Keep a T8132 host's USB devices through a deep sleep, as reset-resume (default: on)");
+
 /*
  * Apple Silicon dwc3 vendor-specific registers
  *
@@ -274,6 +288,21 @@ static void dwc3_apple_set_ptrcap(struct dwc3_apple *appledwc, u32 mode)
 /* Written to the core's GUID scratch register before a sleep in place, read back after it. */
 #define DWC3_APPLE_SLEEP_GUID 0x534c5045
 
+/* dwc3_core_init() takes PHY references; drop them where the core holds its own already. */
+static void dwc3_apple_put_init_refs(struct dwc3 *dwc)
+{
+	int i;
+
+	for (i = 0; i < dwc->num_usb3_ports; i++) {
+		phy_power_off(dwc->usb3_generic_phy[i]);
+		phy_exit(dwc->usb3_generic_phy[i]);
+	}
+	for (i = 0; i < dwc->num_usb2_ports; i++) {
+		phy_power_off(dwc->usb2_generic_phy[i]);
+		phy_exit(dwc->usb2_generic_phy[i]);
+	}
+}
+
 /*
  * The core is powered but its registers are at their reset values. Set it up again the way
  * dwc3_apple_core_start() and dwc3_apple_set_role() do, in place: the PHYs and the xHCI
@@ -283,20 +312,12 @@ static void dwc3_apple_set_ptrcap(struct dwc3_apple *appledwc, u32 mode)
 static int dwc3_apple_core_reinit(struct dwc3_apple *appledwc)
 {
 	struct dwc3 *dwc = &appledwc->dwc;
-	int i, ret;
+	int ret;
 
 	ret = dwc3_core_init(dwc);
 	if (ret)
 		return ret;
-	/* dwc3_core_init() took PHY references the core holds already. */
-	for (i = 0; i < dwc->num_usb3_ports; i++) {
-		phy_power_off(dwc->usb3_generic_phy[i]);
-		phy_exit(dwc->usb3_generic_phy[i]);
-	}
-	for (i = 0; i < dwc->num_usb2_ports; i++) {
-		phy_power_off(dwc->usb2_generic_phy[i]);
-		phy_exit(dwc->usb2_generic_phy[i]);
-	}
+	dwc3_apple_put_init_refs(dwc);
 
 	dwc3_apple_setup_cio(appledwc);
 	dwc3_apple_setup_hibernation(appledwc);
@@ -317,13 +338,19 @@ static int dwc3_apple_core_reinit(struct dwc3_apple *appledwc)
  * be left alone, as an access could be an SError: dwc3_apple_sleep_kept() deals with it once
  * the system is back. A core that came back without its registers is set up again, and xHCI
  * is told it lost its state (a positive return). A sleep refused before the hibernation left
- * the core as it was.
+ * the core as it was. A T8132 host kept registered is dwc3_apple_t8132_reset_resume()'s.
  */
+static int dwc3_apple_t8132_reset_resume(struct dwc3_apple *appledwc);
+
 static int dwc3_apple_pre_host_resume(struct dwc3 *dwc)
 {
 	struct dwc3_apple *appledwc = to_dwc3_apple(dwc);
 	int ret;
 
+	if (READ_ONCE(appledwc->sleep_reset_resume)) {
+		guard(mutex)(&appledwc->lock);
+		return dwc3_apple_t8132_reset_resume(appledwc);
+	}
 	if (!READ_ONCE(appledwc->sleep_in_place))
 		return 0;
 
@@ -661,6 +688,74 @@ static int dwc3_apple_set_role(struct dwc3_apple *appledwc, enum dwc3_apple_stat
 	return 0;
 }
 
+/*
+ * Runs before xHCI touches a T8132 host's core after a deep sleep that left it registered.
+ * A sleep that didn't reach its commit kept the power, and xHCI resumes as usual. One that
+ * did took the PHY and the core down with the SoC (pm_resume_via_firmware()), and the core
+ * may be held in reset, so nothing reads it before the reset is released. The PHY is set up
+ * again (apple_atcphy_sleep_lost()), then the core is brought up the way
+ * dwc3_apple_core_start() and dwc3_apple_set_role() bring up a T8132 host on a cable attach,
+ * with the PHY references the core holds already kept. xHCI is told it lost its state (a
+ * positive return), so it starts over and its devices reset-resume. Any failure keeps xHCI
+ * away and dwc3_apple_reset_resume_kept() restarts the host as after a stop, unless the core
+ * couldn't even be released from reset.
+ */
+static int dwc3_apple_t8132_reset_resume(struct dwc3_apple *appledwc)
+{
+	struct dwc3 *dwc = &appledwc->dwc;
+	bool before_init = dwc3_apple_host_before_core_init(appledwc, DWC3_APPLE_HOST);
+	int lost, ret;
+
+	lockdep_assert_held(&appledwc->lock);
+	if (!pm_resume_via_firmware())
+		return 0;
+
+	appledwc->sleep_lost = true;
+	lost = apple_atcphy_sleep_lost(appledwc->usb2_phy);
+	if (lost)
+		dev_warn(appledwc->dev, "PHY not set up again after the sleep (%d): restarting the host\n",
+			 lost);
+
+	/* T8132 latches the host role while its USB2 PHY is stopped by the reset. */
+	ret = reset_control_assert(appledwc->reset);
+	if (!ret)
+		ret = phy_set_mode(appledwc->usb2_phy, PHY_MODE_USB_HOST);
+	if (!ret)
+		ret = reset_control_deassert(appledwc->reset);
+	if (ret) {
+		/* The core may be held in reset, where an access could be an SError. */
+		appledwc->sleep_dead = true;
+		dev_err(appledwc->dev, "core not released after the sleep (%d): USB off until a reboot\n",
+			ret);
+		return ret;
+	}
+	if (lost)
+		return lost;
+
+	if (before_init) {
+		appledwc->dwc.dr_mode = USB_DR_MODE_HOST;
+		dwc3_apple_set_ptrcap(appledwc, DWC3_GCTL_PRTCAP_HOST);
+	}
+	ret = dwc3_core_init(dwc);
+	if (ret)
+		goto fail;
+	dwc3_apple_put_init_refs(dwc);
+	if (before_init)
+		dwc3_apple_setup_cio(appledwc);
+	dwc3_apple_setup_hibernation(appledwc);
+	ret = dwc3_apple_set_role(appledwc, DWC3_APPLE_HOST);
+	if (ret)
+		goto fail;
+
+	appledwc->sleep_reinit = true;
+	dev_info(appledwc->dev, "power lost in the sleep: host set up again, devices reset-resume\n");
+	return 1;
+
+fail:
+	dev_err(appledwc->dev, "host not set up again after the sleep (%d): restarting it\n", ret);
+	return ret;
+}
+
 static int dwc3_apple_init(struct dwc3_apple *appledwc, enum dwc3_apple_state state)
 {
 	int ret, ret_reset;
@@ -903,8 +998,8 @@ static int dwc3_usb_role_switch_set(struct usb_role_switch *sw, enum usb_role ro
 	if (appledwc->fixed_hub)
 		return 0;
 
-	/* Stopped for a sleep: this is what comes back after it. */
-	if (appledwc->sleep_stopped) {
+	/* Stopped for a sleep, or kept through it: this is what comes back after it. */
+	if (appledwc->sleep_stopped || appledwc->sleep_reset_resume) {
 		appledwc->sleep_state = dwc3_apple_state_for_role(role);
 		return 0;
 	}
@@ -954,7 +1049,8 @@ static enum usb_role dwc3_usb_role_switch_get(struct usb_role_switch *sw)
 
 	guard(mutex)(&appledwc->lock);
 
-	switch (appledwc->sleep_stopped ? appledwc->sleep_state : appledwc->state) {
+	switch (appledwc->sleep_stopped || appledwc->sleep_reset_resume ?
+		appledwc->sleep_state : appledwc->state) {
 	case DWC3_APPLE_HOST:
 		return USB_ROLE_HOST;
 	case DWC3_APPLE_DEVICE:
@@ -1033,15 +1129,65 @@ static bool dwc3_apple_sleep_kept(struct dwc3_apple *appledwc)
 	return false;
 }
 
+/*
+ * After a deep sleep that left a T8132 host registered: kept if the resume set the core up
+ * again, or if the sleep didn't cut the power and the core still has the GUID written before
+ * it. Otherwise restart the host as after a stop, and the devices enumerate again; a core that
+ * couldn't be released from reset is left alone. A role that arrived during the sleep is
+ * taken up now. Returns whether the host and its devices were kept.
+ */
+static bool dwc3_apple_reset_resume_kept(struct dwc3_apple *appledwc)
+{
+	struct dwc3 *dwc = &appledwc->dwc;
+	bool kept = false, lost = appledwc->sleep_lost;
+	u32 guid = 0;
+	int ret;
+
+	appledwc->sleep_reset_resume = false;
+	appledwc->sleep_lost = false;
+	if (appledwc->sleep_reinit) {
+		appledwc->sleep_reinit = false;
+		kept = true;
+	} else if (appledwc->sleep_dead) {
+		return false;
+	} else if (!lost) {
+		/* The power stayed: the core is out of reset and may be read. */
+		guid = dwc3_readl(dwc, DWC3_GUID);
+		if (guid == DWC3_APPLE_SLEEP_GUID) {
+			dwc3_writel(dwc, DWC3_GUID, appledwc->sleep_guid);
+			kept = true;
+		}
+	}
+
+	if (kept && appledwc->sleep_state == DWC3_APPLE_HOST)
+		return true;
+	if (!kept)
+		dev_warn(appledwc->dev,
+			 "host lost its state in the sleep (lost %d, GUID %#x): restarting it\n",
+			 lost, guid);
+	ret = dwc3_apple_exit(appledwc);
+	if (ret) {
+		dev_err(appledwc->dev, "Failed to stop after sleep, err=%d\n", ret);
+		return false;
+	}
+	if (!appledwc->shutting_down && (appledwc->sleep_state == DWC3_APPLE_HOST ||
+					 appledwc->sleep_state == DWC3_APPLE_DEVICE))
+		dwc3_apple_start_after_sleep(appledwc, appledwc->sleep_state);
+
+	return false;
+}
+
 /*
  * Runs before the freezer, when the sleep's real target isn't known yet, so this goes by the
  * state "mem" means now. Writing "freeze" with deep selected costs a USB restart and nothing else.
  * A stop that fails refuses the sleep. PM_POST_SUSPEND follows an abandoned sleep as well.
  */
 static int dwc3_apple_pm_notify_locked(struct dwc3_apple *appledwc, unsigned long action,
-				       bool in_place, bool *finish, bool *kept)
+				       bool in_place, bool lose, bool *finish, bool *kept,
+				       bool *lose_finish)
 {
 	struct device *dev = appledwc->dev;
+	enum dwc3_apple_sleep_plan plan;
 	int ret;
 
 	lockdep_assert_held(&appledwc->lock);
@@ -1057,8 +1203,22 @@ static int dwc3_apple_pm_notify_locked(struct dwc3_apple *appledwc, unsigned lon
 			dev_err(dev, "refusing the sleep: the core is gone until a reboot\n");
 			return notifier_from_errno(-EIO);
 		}
+		plan = dwc3_apple_sleep_plan(appledwc->state, in_place, lose);
+		/* A PHY that agreed to lose the host but isn't asked to is let go again. */
+		*lose_finish = lose && plan != DWC3_APPLE_SLEEP_RESET_RESUME;
+		/*
+		 * A T8132 host stays registered: xHCI suspends as usual, and at the resume
+		 * dwc3_apple_t8132_reset_resume() brings it back. The wakeup setting stays.
+		 */
+		if (plan == DWC3_APPLE_SLEEP_RESET_RESUME) {
+			appledwc->sleep_guid = dwc3_readl(&appledwc->dwc, DWC3_GUID);
+			dwc3_writel(&appledwc->dwc, DWC3_GUID, DWC3_APPLE_SLEEP_GUID);
+			appledwc->sleep_state = DWC3_APPLE_HOST;
+			appledwc->sleep_reset_resume = true;
+			break;
+		}
 		/* xHCI suspends and resumes as usual, and the wakeup setting stays. */
